\[
a^3+b^3=(a+b)(a^2-ab+b^2).
\]

Here \(a=x\) and \(b=4\).  

\[
\begin{aligned}
x^3+64 &= (x+4)\bigl(x^2-x\cdot4+4^2\bigr) \\
&= (x+4)(x^2-4x+16).
\end{aligned}
\]

Now check the quadratic factor \(x^2-4x+16\). Its discriminant is  

\[
\Delta = (-4)^2-4(1)(16)=16-64=-48<0,
\]

so it has no real roots.  
Hence it cannot be factored further over the real numbers.

\[
\boxed{(x+4)(x^2-4x+16)}
\]

