<think>
We need to solve for h(x) given equation: 9x^3 - 3x + 1 + h(x) = 3x^2 - 5x + 3. So h(x) = RHS - LHS. Compute RHS - LHS: 3x^2 -5x +3 - (9x^3 -3x +1) = -9x^3 +3x^2 + ( -5x +3x) = -9x^3 +3x^2 -2x + (3-1) = 2. So h(x) = -9x^3 + 3x^2 - 2x + 2. Terms decreasing degree: -9x^3, +3x^2, -2x, +2. So answer: \boxed{-9x^3 + 3x^2 - 2x + 2}. Put in boxed.
</think>To isolate \(h(x)\) we subtract the polynomial on the left from the polynomial on the right:

\[
h(x)=\bigl(3x^{2}-5x+3\bigr)-\bigl(9x^{3}-3x+1\bigr).
\]

Now combine like terms:

\[
\begin{aligned}
h(x) &= -9x^{3}+3x^{2}+(-5x+3x)+\bigl(3-1\bigr) \\
  &= -9x^{3}+3x^{2}-2x+2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus the polynomial \(h(x)\) in order of decreasing degree is  

\[
\boxed{-9x^{3}+3x^{2}-2x+2}.
